# Explainable AI-Based Federated Hybrid Learning Model for Stroke Prediction

An academic M.Tech dissertation implementation for stroke-risk prediction. It combines a CNN→RNN→LSTM hybrid model, simulated federated learning, SHAP explanations, dynamic risk assessment, personalized recommendations, Flask, SQLite, and a Bootstrap frontend.

> This is an educational prototype. Its output is not a medical diagnosis and it must not be used as production clinical software.

## Implemented dissertation features

- Stroke prediction from the Kaggle healthcare stroke dataset
- Missing-value handling, encoding, scaling, SMOTE, and train/test split
- CNN→RNN→LSTM hybrid deep-learning architecture
- Early stopping, checkpointing, and learning-rate reduction
- Evaluation against Decision Tree, Random Forest, Logistic Regression, and SVM
- Three-hospital federated-learning simulation with weighted aggregation
- SHAP summary, bar, force, waterfall, and dependence explanations
- Low, Medium, and High dynamic risk assessment
- Personalized diet, exercise, doctor-visit, lifestyle, and medication reminders
- Flask APIs, SQLite history, and responsive Bootstrap pages
- Automated tests for the completed application workflow
- Custom hospital letterhead, four report themes, logo/signature uploads, and local PDF export
- Doctor remarks, recommended tests, follow-up reminders, prediction timeline, health score, and lifestyle-impact analysis

## Quick start

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python run.py
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:5000`. Register a demonstration account, log in, and open the Prediction page.

The required dataset file is:

```text
dataset/healthcare-dataset-stroke-data.csv
```

Official source: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/fedesoriano/stroke-prediction-dataset

## Main academic commands

```powershell
python -m utils.data_loader
python -m utils.preprocessing
python -m utils.eda
python train.py
python -m utils.evaluation
python -m federated.server
python -m xai.shap_explainer
python -m tests.test_phase14
python -m tests.test_phase16
```

Training and SHAP generation can take several minutes. The Flask application uses the already saved artifacts in `models/` and `reports/`.

